# Conventional Commits (required)

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) in **English** for commits and PR titles.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>
```

## Types and semver (python-semantic-release on `main`)

| Type | Release |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | Minor |
| `fix`, `perf`, `refactor`, `revert`, `build` | Patch |
| `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `style`, `test` | No bump (CI may still apply fallback patch if nothing else released) |

Breaking: `feat!` or footer `BREAKING CHANGE:` → major.

## Parser fallback

`scripts/datapyn_commit_parser.py` also maps imperative subjects without a prefix (e.g. `Add …` → minor, `Improve …` / `Delete …` → patch) and parses GitHub merge commit bodies. Prefer explicit `feat:` / `fix:` anyway.

## Agent obligations

1. Propose and use `type(scope): subject` for every commit and PR title.
2. User-facing work → `feat` or `fix`, not bare `Improve …` or `chore`.
3. Do not use free-form merge titles when a release is expected.

Examples: `feat(copilot): add image attachments`, `fix(results): format epoch datetimes`, `ci(release): improve PSR fallback`

Full guide: `.github/git-commit-instructions.md`
